year>2018</year></date></event><event event-type="pmc-release"><date><day>28</day><month>12</month><year>2018</year></date></event><event event-type="pmc-live"><date><day>28</day><month>12</month><year>2018</year></date></event><event event-type="pmc-last-change"><date iso-8601-date="2024-06-18 14:25:28.847"><day>18</day><month>06</month><year>2024</year></date></event></pub-history><permissions><license license-type="permissions-link"><license-p><bold>PERMISSIONS</bold><ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="http://www.sciencemag.org/help/reprints-and-permissions">http://www.sciencemag.org/help/reprints-and-permissions</ext-link></license-p></license></permissions><self-uri xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" content-type="pmc-pdf" xlink:href="nihms-987921.pdf"/><abstract id="ABS1"><p id="P1">Insulin-like growth factor 2 (IGF2) is the major fetal growth hormone in mammals. We identify zinc finger protein 568 (ZFP568), a member of the rapidly evolving Kruppel-associated box-zinc finger protein (KRAB-ZFP) family linked primarily to silencing of endogenous retroelements, as a direct repressor of a placental-specific <italic toggle="yes">Igf2</italic> transcript (designated <italic toggle="yes">Igf2-P0</italic>) in mice. Loss of <italic toggle="yes">Zfp568</italic>, which causes gastrulation failure, or mutation of the ZFP568-binding site at the <italic toggle="yes">Igf2-P0</italic> promoter causes inappropriate <italic toggle="yes">Igf2-P0</italic> activation. Deletion of <italic toggle="yes">Igf2</italic> can completely rescue <italic toggle="yes">Zfp568</italic> gastrulation phenotypes through late gestation. Our data highlight the exquisite selectivity with which members of the KRAB-ZFP family repress their targets and identify an additional layer of transcriptional control of a key growth factor regulating fetal and placental development.</p></abstract><custom-meta-group><custom-meta><meta-name>pmc-status-qastatu